# SOP-LMS-030 External Training Equivalency and Exemptions

## 1. Purpose

Describes how training completed outside {{ org.short }} is evaluated as equivalent to a required course (POL-CT-001 section 9), and how exemptions are recorded.

## 2. Equivalency Request

2.1 The employee or manager submits an equivalency request in the LMS with the certificate, course outline and completion date.

2.2 Reviewers: EHS for safety courses (EHS-*), the Privacy Officer for HIPAA courses (PRV-*), HR for harassment prevention courses (HR-*). Reviewers decide within 10 business days.

2.3 Approval criteria: the external course covers the same learning objectives as the {{ org.short }} course; it was completed within the recertification interval of the {{ org.short }} course; and the certificate identifies the provider, learner and completion date.

2.4 Approved equivalencies are recorded as an "External Equivalent" completion with the original completion date, so the next due date is calculated from that date.

## 3. Items That Cannot Be Satisfied Externally

- EHS-EAP-100 Site Emergency Action Plan (site-specific).
- EHS-FL-201 Forklift Practical Evaluation (must be performed in the {{ org.short }} workplace on {{ org.short }} equipment).
- PRV-HIP-110 HIPAA Privacy Essentials ({{ org.short }}'s own policies and procedures).
- EHS-LOTO-150 practical component (machine-specific energy control procedures).

## 4. Harassment Prevention Training from a Prior Employer

4.1 California employees hired with a certificate of harassment prevention training completed in the previous two years may submit it for equivalency. If approved, the next HR-HAR-100 or HR-HAR-200 assignment is due two years from the prior completion date. The employee still acknowledges {{ org.short }}'s anti-harassment policy within {{ policy.current.new_hire_window_days }} days.

## 5. Exemptions

5.1 An exemption removes a requirement for an employee who does not perform the related task (for example a warehouse operator who is permanently assigned to non-powered equipment). Exemptions require manager and EHS approval, expire after 12 months, and are reviewed at each job change.
